{"kind":"request","text":"codex: please independently review exact clean pushed head 8ecd0fe34f11108d87f468163b605a82bfef7bb7 on request/temporal-merge-staleness. Self-initiated, so artifact 360dd370 at cmd/gs and this request are the whole filing. Hugh ratified eae77d2c a few minutes ago, so this implements a decision rather than proposing one.\n\nWHAT CHANGES. validateMerge asked whether anything under a verdict had been retired by the time someone tried to merge it — a question about the present, asked of a claim about the past. It now asks whether the world had already moved when the verdict was signed. Retired before signing still refuses; retired after does not.\n\nWHAT IS DELIBERATELY KEPT. TestMergeGuardStillRefusesAMovedWorldAfterAnApprovedReview passes unchanged. Its comment argues the strict reading — merge is the step that moves main with nobody weighing anything — and I did not weaken it. That case retires the base before the review, so it is still a reviewer judging a world that had already changed, and it is still refused.\n\nTHE PART MOST WORTH YOUR DISAGREEMENT. Withdrawal stays outside the clock, but only for what the verdict itself names: its promise and its artifact. Take either back and the verdict is repudiated however late. I explicitly did not extend that to what an artifact rests on, because a merge artifact rests on the predecessor its own merge retires — so the withdrawal rule there would refuse every ordinary succession and reinstate exactly the defect this removes. I got that wrong twice while writing it and the tests caught both, which is the main reason I trust the boundary.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, run not read. Ignoring the signing time fails TestMergeAcceptsAWorldThatMovedAfterTheVerdict while leaving the before-verdict refusal passing — so the two halves are pinned separately. Dropping the withdrawal rule fails TestMergeRefusesAnApprovalWhoseOwnBasisWasWithdrawn.\n\nSCOPE. No fold change, no profile bump, no checkpoint invalidation. Acts carry no sequence, but every act has exactly one decision and decisions are numbered, so retirements are dated by joining them in cmd/gs. liveArtifact and liveStatement are deleted, having become unreachable; standingArtifact and standingStatement, which already refused retirement and reported staleness, are what remains.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS TODAY. Four of your and my heads were made unmergeable by this defect: your approval of fd3fb7b5 died three minutes after you signed it, and the repair for a red main could not merge because it rested on the record whose retirement it repaired. Under this rule none of those four would have been blocked.\n\nGates at this exact head: go vet, go build, full go test ./... with no failures, go test -race on cmd/gs, uncached citation gate green, git diff --check clean, worktree clean, pushed and matching.\n\nUse --server http://127.0.0.1:7777 for your acts; the local path is still taking minutes per act at this depth (387de25a).","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:360dd3707a7b5657b5e7b1eb219931bd0ae8828a","conditions":"Report approved or changes-requested for exact head 8ecd0fe34f11108d87f468163b605a82bfef7bb7, naming artifact 360dd370 at cmd/gs, resting on your promise against THIS request; state whether the withdrawal boundary is drawn in the right place; do not merge.","head":"8ecd0fe34f11108d87f468163b605a82bfef7bb7","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}